<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lyrical Ballads</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1798">1798</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Lyrical Ballads, with a Few Other Poems</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lyrical Ballads is a collection of poems by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-wordsworth">William Wordsworth</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-taylor-coleridge">Samuel Taylor Coleridge</a>, first published in 1798 and generally considered to have marked the beginning of the English Romantic movement. Coleridge's four poems in the first edition, about a third of its length, included The Rime of the Ancient Mariner; the other nineteen were Wordsworth's. The poets set out to overturn what they considered the learned, highly sculpted forms of 18th-century English poetry and to write in common, everyday language. Wordsworth's preface to the 1800 edition became known as the manifesto of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanticism">Romanticism</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lyrical-ballads.jpg" alt="Lyrical Ballads" width="320" height="528" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: William Wordsworth (1770-1850) and Samuel Taylor Coleridge (1772-1834). · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lyrical_Ballads.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Lyrical Ballads, with a Few Other Poems was published in 1798. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-wordsworth">Wordsworth</a> wrote nineteen of the poems and Coleridge four, which together made up about a third of the book in length. Coleridge's contributions included one of his best-known works, The Rime of the Ancient Mariner. The immediate effect on critics was modest, but the book became a landmark that changed the course of English poetry. A second edition appeared in 1800, in which Wordsworth added further poems and a preface setting out the pair's poetical principles. A third edition followed in 1802 with substantial additions to the preface, and a fourth in 1805; one edition of 1802 also carried an appendix titled Poetic Diction.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-wordsworth">Wordsworth</a> and Coleridge set out to replace what they considered the priggish, learned and highly sculpted forms of eighteenth-century poetry with verse in common, everyday language, accessible to the average person. The title itself recalls rustic forms: lyrical links the poems with ancient rustic bards and suggests spontaneity, while ballads are an oral mode of storytelling used by common people. In the Advertisement to the 1798 edition Wordsworth called most of the poems experiments, written to ascertain how far the language of conversation in the middle and lower classes was suited to poetic pleasure.</p><p>The choice of simple country people as the subject of poetry was an even greater shift than the language. One of the main themes is a return to a state of nature in which people lived a purer and more innocent life; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-wordsworth">Wordsworth</a> shared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-rousseau">Rousseau</a>'s belief that humanity was essentially good but corrupted by society.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The preface to the 1800 edition became known as the manifesto of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanticism">Romanticism</a>. For it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-wordsworth">Wordsworth</a> added the poems of a second volume, among them Michael, Ruth and the Lucy poems. He left out The Convict, which had been in 1798, because of its politically controversial subject, which reflected his opposition to the death penalty and harsh prison conditions; Coleridge's Love took its place. Lines Written a Few Miles above Tintern Abbey closed the first volume.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lyrical Ballads” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In his preface to the second edition of <b>Lyrical Ballads</b> in 1800 Wordsworth declared that “all good poetry is the spontaneous overflow of powerful feelings” and sought poetry in ordinary life and in the language people really speak, while Samuel Taylor Coleridge gave the supernatural a new imaginative…</blockquote><a href="/movement/romanticism">Romanticism · Aims</a></li></ul></section>
